ence":90,"evidenceIds":["E12","E2"]},{"itemId":"I23","answer":"sufficient","confidence":82,"evidenceIds":["E8","E9","E10","E14","E15"]}],"evidenceRequests":["Q3","Q1","Q2"],"summary":"The on-time decline is real, confirmed by independent manual ride checks. It tracks two operational changes: cutting recovery time on 21 routes in April, and lane reductions on five corridor routes. Group C, with neither change, held steady, and Groups A and B stepped down at the times those changes took effect. The 40% absence rise is mostly a recording artifact. ShiftLine began logging partial-day absences as occurrences, which account for 238 of the 245-occurrence monthly increase. Full-day absences rose only 1.1%. The independent dispatch log shows unchanged unfilled trips and pull-out lateness. The proposed attendance discipline would punish operators for a measurement change and for schedule-driven early leaves. Restore recovery time, set construction running times, and restate the absence data first."}}